b:
This is part of an email sent out from a local business on a coastal island:

&gt; First the good news!
&gt; Well, it got damned close, but we survived.
&gt; 
&gt; We are open for business! [Business Name 1] is still running Quad Tours and [Business Name 2] still has awesome Sandboarding and Guided Fat Bike Tours.
&gt; 
&gt; After weeks of continuous attacks, from multiple fire fronts, we feel like we have dodged a massive bullet! Both our businesses, [Business Name 2] and [Business Name 1] suffered fire damage but not total destruction and all of our tours and activities are still fully operating.
&gt; 
&gt; Since late December, [Owner Name] has spent every day fighting fires around the Island helping family, friends and the community save what they could of their beloved island. Meanwhile [Spouse Name], and the three boys, have lived through some very long and stressful days, evacuating three times, going days without seeing [Owner Name] and with no phone communication when he was on the fire ground, often unsure if he was safe. You may well have seen some of [Owner Name]'s footage that he took of the firestorm that he, [Colleague Name] and their dad [Father's Name] were caught in - it was picked up by the world's media. You can watch it above.
&gt; 
&gt; Yesterday it finally rained and was the first day we weren't active on the fire ground and could take stock of our situation. 
&gt; It's not all good news!
&gt; Yes, we survived and our business is still going, but [Island Name] tourism has taken an almighty hit in what should have been our busiest month of the year.
&gt; 
&gt; Like many other tourism businesses on the island our turnover is 80-90% down at the moment. We have sadly had to let go of some great staff (luckily they have all found jobs elsewhere on the mainland) and are doing whatever we can to get through this rough period. This is the time of year we would normally be saving money to get us through the quieter winter months ahead - but right now, we are managing to pay our staff and not much else.
&gt; 
&gt; We might have survived the fires, but we're not sure we will survive the aftermath.
&gt; 
&gt; Trust us, this is not easy for us to say, but...
&gt; 
&gt; WE ARE GOING TO NEED SOME HELP.

[Owner Name] owns the business with his wife. He, literally, has spent every day during what's the busiest time of the year for his business working with out pay to fight the fires. [This is the video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fQAHupUy9w8) mentioned in the text above.
